AR/tarball/v0.6.9","zipball_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanenRac/ARMOR-NODE-SOLAR/zipball/v0.6.9","body":"- **The shared project tool is the current one:** the copy follows the shared repository, which now knows the new solar panel node.\r\n\r\n- **The Bluetooth PIN derived from the setup code is made with HMAC-SHA256:** the first four bytes of HMAC-SHA256 (setup code as key, `armor-ble-passkey` as message), modulo one million. **The PIN of this node changes** with this firmware (the setup code does not): read it again on the USB console or in the panel. A fixed or random PIN is as before. See `docs/SETUP_CODE.md`.\r\n- **The capacity of a Pylontech stack is told by batteries that print no ampere-hours:** when neither the `bat` table nor `info` gives the charge left, it is the module's state of charge applied to its capacity, so the stack's remaining and total capacity and its energy appear. When the cells of a `bat` table give different charges, the module's is the lowest (the one a real US2000 reports itself) instead of the first cell's. An `info` that does not answer no longer erases the capacity it told before, and a capacity still unknown is asked again after five minutes.\r\n- **All the batteries of a stack are read, up to sixteen:** with the port's setting at 0 the node asked only the first eight cell by cell, so from the ninth on there were no cells and no capacity (and the stack no totals). A port with a lower limit now says so on the Readings page. A stack of eight or fewer takes the same time as before.\r\n- **The state of charge of the whole system is weighted by capacity:** a stack of one battery no longer weighs as much as a stack of six; with several stacks and one that does not say its full capacity, no total is sent.\r\n- **The limits that mark a battery as faulty are settings of the node:** cell low and high, the spread between cells and the temperature (the same defaults as before), edited on the Ports page in eight languages and checked for their order.\r\n- **An inverter that answers `QPIGS` is no longer left unread:** a unit that never answers `QMOD` or `QPIWS` (silence, `NAK` or a reply that cannot be read) was never published at all; from the third try the reading goes out with the mode `unknown` or `warnings_known` false. A warning list longer than the documented 36 flags is accepted. Tried on a computer only.\r\n- **The raw view of a port says why nothing is read:** it starts with the last question and its bytes, the bytes sent and heard, the answers, refusals and silences, and the last error. The hint of a silent inverter port and the wiring notes now give the level converter and the inverter's own pinout (not the one of the Pylontech sockets).\r\n- **More equipment can be read, listed in one catalogue of protocols:** new port types for Modbus inverters and chargers (EASun SMG II, SRNE), DALY, JBD and JK battery boards and Victron VE.Direct, and the PI41 and PI34 variants of the Voltronic type. Written from the public documents and tried on a computer; none has been tried on real equipment. A temperature or a cell range the equipment does not give is left out of the message instead of being sent as zero.\r\n- **Three and four battery stacks no longer use up the internal memory:** the tasks that read the ports, and the web server while it builds the status and the list of ports, now take their C++ data from the external memory (PSRAM); before, the many small strings and vectors of every battery (about 25 to 35 kB for a port with eight batteries) went to the internal memory, which ran low with a third stack and left the web panel unanswered with a fourth. The counters of each battery no longer keep a copy of their names, the last message of a port is kept once instead of three times, and an emulated port's ring of edges takes 8 kB instead of 16. Not tried on a board.\r\n- **The readings leave the node one port at a time:** `GET /api/v1/readings` is sent in pieces without joining the messages, `?port=N` gives one port, and `status` and `ports` no longer repeat each port's whole reading (`ports[].reading` is gone). The panel asks for one thing after another, only for the ports with a new reading, and stops asking while its tab is hidden.\r\n- **A new Readings page:** the whole system added up, one section for each port, a card for each battery with its 15 or 16 cells as bars coloured by voltage, the spread between cells, state of charge, current, temperatures, health and the last event, and a soft blink (under two a second, none for a person who asks for less movement) on a fault or an alarm; the inverter as tiles. In the eight languages, with no library, its look in `panel/readings.css`.\r\n- **The overview shows the internal memory:** free now, the least it has been and the largest block.\r\n- **A stack's capacity is the whole stack's or it is left out:** `capacity_ah`, `energy_kwh` and `full_capacity_ah` used to be the sum of only the batteries that had told theirs, which read as a half empty or a smaller bank.\r\n- **A host test limits the memory of each stack** (`tests/test_memory.cpp`: four ports of eight batteries of 15 and 16 cells) and `tests/check_memory_routing.py` keeps the rules above in place.\r\n- **The contracts it says it was checked against are 0.8.1.6:** the version that the node reports as the one of the contracts follows the pin of the shared repository after its latest green run of the reverse dependencies.\r\n\r\n- **The screenshots of the README can be taken on a GitHub runner:** the manual workflow `screenshots.yml` (started by hand only, read-only token, no secrets) starts the stand-in of the node, takes the pages of the web panel with the Chromium of Playwright and keeps the pictures as an artifact; `ARMOR_SHOTS_CHANNEL=chromium` makes the script take that Chromium; `docs/SCREENSHOTS.md` says how to start it and use them.\r\n- **The contracts it says it was checked against are 0.8.1.5:** the information message of the node carries the version of the contracts that the reverse-dependency run of ARMOR-COMMON last checked it against.\r\n"}
